# generated-by: sutra create app (edit freely — this file is yours)
#
# Sealed archives are BUILD OUTPUT, not source. `sutra package packages/<pkg>` derives one from
# the package directory — manifest, per-file digests and the content-addressed deployment id are
# all computed at seal time — and dropping it here is what deploys it. Committing one would
# check in a binary that changes on every rebuild and can never be reviewed, while the thing it
# was built from (packages/) is already in the repository.
deployments/*.sutra

# The engine service reads this file (`env_file:` in compose.yaml) to resolve the secrets your
# packages name by reference — `env:MY_API_KEY`, `env:MY_DB_URL`. It therefore holds credentials
# by design, dev ones today and easily a real one by accident tomorrow, so it stays out of git.
# Share the KEY NAMES with your team (a committed `.env.example` is the usual way); never the
# values. Nothing breaks without the file — compose declares it `required: false`.
.env
